   Who is it for? Businesses with up to about fifty staff without an IT or legal department, anywhere in the EU, that are asked for security evidence: by a larger customer, a contract, an insurer or a label. The generator asks for your country and the handbook has notes for Austria and Germany. My customer sent a questionnaire with its own questions. Does this answer it? Mostly. Questionnaires ask the same things in different words. The self-declaration follows the ten areas of Article 21(2) of the NIS 2 Directive (EUR-Lex, retrieved 2026-10-23), which customers covered by it often use to structure their questionnaires, and the mapping in D5 shows which section answers which typical question. What your customer asks beyond that, you answer with the same four honest levels. What if the honest answer is "not yet"? Then you say so, with a date. "Not yet, by the end of the quarter, responsible: the office manager with the IT provider" is a complete answer; the handbook explains why the law that covers your customer expects exactly that of the customer itself. The action plan carries the date, the cover letter names it. Does it give us a certificate or a label? No. It gives you a self-declaration with evidence, which is what most customers and insurers ask for first. If someone wants an external check, the handbook says which one fits a small business: the CyberRisikoCheck in Germany, the Standard label in Austria, and why ISO 27001 is rarely the first step. Do I need my IT provider? For the technical measures, yes: multi-factor authentication, backups, updates, the router. The action plan marks these and names the provider next to you. The organisational measures, the rules, the lists and the emergency card, you do yourself. What happens when the law or a questionnaire changes? We update the templates and the handbook during your licence. Your evidence folder shows which frozen versions predate an update, so you know what to redo. What do you store about me? Your email address, your answers and the runs of the generator. The generator never asks for passwords, access data or IP addresses. Names and phone numbers appear only where you enter them for the cover letter and the emergency card: the contact person, the signatory, the IT provider and the recipient; incidents appear only as a level. No tracking cookies; page views are counted without any personal data. 


